class Solution:
    def maxDistance(self, side: int, points: List[List[int]], k: int) -> int:


=== MODEL ===
This is a known approach: map each boundary point to a 1D perimeter coordinate, then binary search on the answer D. For a given D, check whether we can pick k points on the cycle with consecutive (cyclic) gaps ≥ D, using a greedy from each starting point. Note that Manhattan distance between boundary points on a square is not simply the perimeter gap, so I need to be careful about correctness. The standard reduction is that for boundary points, the Manhattan distance equals the perimeter-based distance measured as min over the two directions only in specific cases, so I'll use the direct pairwise check within the greedy instead of relying on perimeter distance.

I'll implement: binary search D, and for feasibility, greedily build a selection in perimeter order starting from each candidate, requiring every newly chosen point to have Manhattan distance ≥ D from all previously chosen points.
TOOL submit_solution {"ticket_id": "Q-3554", "code": "from typing import List\n\nclass Solution:\n    def maxDistance(self, side: int, points: List[List[int]], k: int) -> int:\n        def perim(p):\n            x, y = p\n            if y == 0:\n                return x\n            if x == side:\n                return side + y\n            if y == side:\n                return 3 * side - x\n            return 4 * side - y  # x == 0\n\n        pts = sorted(points, key=perim)\n        n = len(pts)\n\n        def md(a, b):\n            return abs(a[0] - b[0]) + abs(a[1] - b[1])\n\n        def feasible(D):\n            # Greedy from each start index, choosing points in perimeter order\n            # such that each new point is at distance >= D from all chosen ones.\n            for s in range(n):\n                chosen = [pts[s]]\n                for j in range(1, n):\n                    if len(chosen) == k:\n                        break\n                    cand = pts[(s + j) % n]\n                    ok = True\n                    for c in chosen:\n                        if md(c, cand) < D:\n                            ok = False\n                            break\n                    if ok:\n                        chosen.append(cand)\n                if len(chosen) >= k:\n                    return True\n            return False\n\n        lo, hi = 0, 2 * side\n        while lo < hi:\n            mid = (lo + hi + 1) // 2\n            if feasible(mid):\n                lo = mid\n            else:\n                hi = mid - 1\n        return lo\n"}

