class Solution:
    def maxDistance(self, side: int, points: List[List[int]], k: int) -> int:


=== MODEL ===
This is the "max min distance on square boundary" problem. Approach:

1. Map each boundary point to a 1D perimeter coordinate t in [0, 4*side), going clockwise from (0,0). On the boundary, the Manhattan distance between two points is at least... the standard trick is that for boundary points, the Manhattan distance equals the min of the perimeter distance wrapped around in a way that works with the 1D cyclic ordering. Concretely, I use the known fact that the maximum-min-distance selection on the boundary reduces to picking k points on a cycle of length P = 4*side where consecutive gaps are measured by Manhattan distance, which for points on the boundary is min(|t_i - t_j|, P - |t_i - t_j|) only when they are on the same or adjacent sides; the safe approach is to compute the actual Manhattan distance for each pair during the check.

2. Binary search on the answer d. For a candidate d, check whether we can pick k points with all pairwise Manhattan distances >= d: sort points by perimeter coordinate, fix each point as the starting point, then greedily pick the next point in cyclic order that is at Manhattan distance >= d from all already-picked points (greedy by perimeter order, verifying pairwise distances against chosen points to be safe), and see if we reach k.

3. Return the largest d that passes.
TOOL submit_solution {"ticket_id": "Q-3554", "code": "from typing import List\n\nclass Solution:\n    def maxDistance(self, side: int, points: List[List[int]], k: int) -> int:\n        P = 4 * side\n\n        def perim(x, y):\n            # clockwise from (0,0): bottom edge, right edge, top edge, left edge\n            if y == 0:\n                return x\n            if x == side:\n                return side + y\n            if y == side:\n                return 3 * side - x\n            return 4 * side - y  # x == 0\n\n        pts = sorted(((perim(x, y), x, y) for x, y in points))\n        n = len(pts)\n\n        def md(a, b):\n            return abs(a[1] - b[1]) + abs(a[2] - b[2])\n\n        def feasible(d):\n            # try each starting point, greedily pick in cyclic perimeter order\n            for s in range(n):\n                chosen = [pts[s]]\n                for off in range(1, n):\n                    cand = pts[(s + off) % n]\n                    ok = True\n                    for c in chosen:\n                        if md(c, cand) < d:\n                            ok = False\n                            break\n                    if ok:\n                        chosen.append(cand)\n                        if len(chosen) == k:\n                            return True\n            return False\n\n        lo, hi = 0, side * 2\n        while lo < hi:\n            mid = (lo + hi + 1) // 2\n            if feasible(mid):\n                lo = mid\n            else:\n                hi = mid - 1\n        return lo\n"}

